LTGM: Draft with the Sanctum Showdown style scoring has completed.
Here are my notes in no particular order.

Breaking this down for those of you without more than a decade of limited card pool game modes (sealed, draft, etc) experience in other card games, the limited card pool game modes typically fall under one of three Card Pool Quality Categories and one of two Card Pool Quantity Categories.

Card Pool Quality Categories:
(1) Limited Treasure - Everything is great.
(2) Normal - Everything is normal.
(3) Limited Trash - Everything is awful.

Card Pool Quantity Categories:
(1) Draft - Pick 1 from a pool, then pass the remainder.
(2) Sealed - Each player gets the same number of cards, but each player's respective pool is random.

The current iteration of Marvel Snap's draft is Draft: Limited Trash.

When the complete card pool has extremely rare cards that are extremely good (aka, "bombs"), whoever pulls more bombs wins.  You still have to draw it, play it, etc, but stats are stats and stats wins games.  When the quality category is Limited Treasure, everything is good, and the difference between the average card in your deck vs bombs has a smaller spread, so the impact is less.  When the average card in your deck is trash, like in Draft: Limited Trash, that spread is appreciably larger.

Chaffe refers to the average card give or take a small spread.
Trash refers to bad cards you have to play to fill out your minimum deck size requirements.
Specifically, in Limited /Trash/ formats, the term Trash is synonymous with the term Chaffe.

You start with 7 synergy trash picks, 1 off pick, and 2 good picks (chance of bomb).

Top Picks
These are my general top picks for the format.  If you see these cards in your starter set, you should strongly consider picking their set, even with bad cards, over other "good" Starter packs.

(1) Nico Minoru
If we are judging cards by their respective upper bounds and including ease of combo, Nico Minoru is probably the single best card in the format.  Nico is an auto-pick with regeneration because her Add a Copy effect pairs game-breakingly well with cards that Destroy her, including cards with Augment: Salvage.  The two-card combo of Venom + Nico (with Agument: Regeneration) can solo all three lanes with relative, albeit somewhat random, ease due to scaling.

(2) Lasher
Lasher is probably the most underrated top pick on my list.  It is cheap, included in Boost, and, if you can pull it off, effectively disables Deadpool, an MVP in the Destroy starter.  It can also swing a lane with small investment.

(3) Ironheart
When Ironheart has a Power-buff, she becomes a top pick.  When you already have other top pick cards, her On Reveal can become exponential.  She also pairs well with many Location and Augment synergies.

(4) Black Panther
There are too many good Location, Augment, and Card Upgrade combinations to ever pass on Black Panther.  He can single-handedly solo a lane from way, way behind.

(5) Namor
If you can maximize Namor, she is worth 17 Power across three lanes.  If you Augment her with Mentor, she is worth 27 across four lanes, the fourth being the top character card of your deck.  To my knowledge, only Black Panther exceeds this power ceiling without supplemental combos.

(6) Isca the Unbeaten
Isca the Unbeaten with Augment: Salvage is top tier.

(7) America Chavez
Cheap early lane control with a significant buff to boot.  Her total power rivals Martyr.  Adding Augment: Encore puts her Total Effective Power at 8.  Since this variant of the Draft uses the Sanctum Showdown scoring, cheap, early pressure provides valuable early points to help close out a game in the mid-game before the late-game decks' synergies go into full swing.

(8) Majestic Wingbeat
Arguably one of the most efficient boosts in the game for both Power and Cost with a cheap, flexible cost.

(9) Knull
Arguably the best 4-Cost in the game.  Yes, I said 4-Cost, not 6-Cost.  One of the options in the late deck-building phase gives -2 Cost or +4 Power.  -2 Cost on Knull in Destroy is far better than -2 Cost or +4 Power on any other 6-Cost.

(10) Juggernaut
When the mid- and late-game boils down to, "Chase the rabbit," knocking enemy characters away from a key position will win the game.  Even mid-tier skilldiff players can realize this opportunity.

Deck / Metagame
There is an entire discussion to be had about approaching Limited games from a Constructed perspective to determine how to determine and just pick orders, but that is for another day.  For now, it is important to understand the Magic: the Gathering deck archtype Sligh.  Sligh uses cheap creatures and efficient removal, both usually with significant drawbacks, to close out the game early.  The Sanctum Showdown scoring favors this archtype heavily.  Ideally, you capture points early, and craft your lanes to control or two lanes on a significant turn in the mid-game and win with a snap.

The closest archtype in Snap to MTG's Sligh is, "Zoo."  It's not the same, but that is the starting point for any new players.

Specifically, a lot of the "bad" cards in Draft don't really have drawbacks in the traditional sense, so you're just looking for cheap, efficient creatures where you can capitalize faster and more efficiently than your opponent without locking yourself entirely out of any specific lane.

You do not care how many points you opponent has as long as you win.  It is a resource like any other.  They can even have 18 or more points (if any player ends the turn with at least 18 points the game ends) as long as you have more.
